faq:
  - q: Is a trip time charter the same as a voyage charter?
    a: >-
      No. A trip time charter (TCT) covers one voyage, but it is priced as daily
      hire and the charterer pays the bunkers and port costs, as under any time
      charter. It suits charterers who want to control the voyage, or who expect
      waiting or extra port calls that would be expensive as demurrage.
  - q: Who pays port costs and bunkers under a time charter?
    a: >-
      The charterer. Under a time charter the owner provides the ship and crew
      and pays the running costs (crew, maintenance, insurance of the hull and
      the owner's liabilities); the charterer pays hire plus the voyage costs:
      bunkers, port and canal charges, and agency fees.
  - q: Does demurrage apply to a time charter?
    a: >-
      Not between owner and time charterer: port time is simply paid as hire. A
      time charterer who sub-lets the ship on voyage terms will, however, earn or
      pay demurrage under that voyage charter.
  - q: When does a contract of affreightment make sense?
    a: >-
      When you have a regular, reasonably predictable flow of cargoes on similar
      routes but do not want to operate ships. A rule of thumb our LPG desk uses:
      if more than half of a year's cargoes are predictable, a COA or a time
      charter usually beats fixing every cargo spot.
  - q: Can I mix structures?
    a: >-
      Yes, and most regular charterers do: a core of COA or period tonnage for the
      predictable volume, and the spot market for peaks, delays and one-off
      cargoes. The mix is reviewed as the market and your programme change.
