Use a **voyage charter** when you have one cargo to move, a **time charter** when you want control of a ship for a period, and a **contract of affreightment (COA)** when you have a series of cargoes but do not want to run ships. Under a voyage charter the owner pays the voyage costs and you pay freight; under a time charter you pay daily hire and the voyage costs yourself; under a COA the owner commits to carry an agreed volume over months or years and chooses which ship performs each cargo.

The right answer depends on three things: how regular your cargoes are, how much freight-market risk you want to carry, and whether you have the operations team to run a ship. The rest of this guide explains each structure as tanker and gas-carrier brokers use it every day, and where each one tends to go wrong.

## How do the three structures compare?

{% routeTable caption="Who pays what, and who carries which risk" %}
{% table %}
-
- Voyage charter
- Time charter
- COA
---
- Price basis
- Freight per tonne, in Worldscale points or as a lump sum
- Hire per day (per month for many gas carriers)
- Freight per tonne for each cargo, fixed or adjusted by an agreed formula
---
- Bunkers, port and canal costs
- Owner
- Charterer
- Owner
---
- Speed and weather at sea
- Owner
- Charterer (protected by the speed and consumption warranty)
- Owner
---
- Time in port
- Charterer, beyond the agreed laytime (demurrage)
- Charterer (hire keeps running)
- Charterer, beyond laytime, cargo by cargo
---
- Control of the ship
- None beyond the voyage
- Full commercial control within the charter's limits
- None; the owner nominates ships
---
- Best for
- Single or irregular cargoes
- Regular programmes, trading optionality
- Regular volumes without operating ships
{% /table %}
{% /routeTable %}

## What is a voyage charter?

On a voyage charter the owner agrees to carry a stated cargo between stated ports for an agreed freight. Tanker freight is usually quoted in Worldscale points or as a lump sum, and gas-carrier freight in dollars per tonne. The owner runs the voyage and pays for it: bunkers, port charges, canal and Straits dues, agency. Time at sea, including bad weather or waiting for a transit on the way, is the owner's problem, because it only reduces the owner's earnings.

Your exposure as charterer is **time in port**. The charter party allows a period of laytime for loading and discharging; once it is used up, you pay demurrage at a daily rate agreed in the fixture. That is why the laytime clause, the notice of readiness rules and the demurrage time bar matter more than most people expect. Our [laytime and demurrage guide](/blog/laytime-and-demurrage-a-practical-guide-for-tanker-and-lpg-charters) walks through a calculation.

Typical pitfalls on voyage charters:

- **Quantity terms left loose.** State the quantity and the tolerance, and whose option it is. A short-loaded cargo can mean dead freight.
- **Terminal restrictions discovered late.** A ship that fits the freight idea but not the berth (length, draft, manifold, vetting) costs time to replace.
- **Demurrage claims lost on paperwork.** Most tanker forms and riders set a time bar for presenting a claim with its documents; missing it loses the claim, however good it is.

## What is a time charter?

On a time charter you hire the ship, with its crew, for a period or for a trip, and pay hire, normally in advance. The owner keeps paying for the ship itself: crew, maintenance, insurance and technical management. You decide where the ship goes within the trading limits, and you pay the voyage costs: bunkers, port and canal charges and agency.

Because you pay for the time, the owner warrants the ship's **speed and consumption**, and the charter sets out when the ship goes **off hire** (for example a breakdown that stops it working). Most disputes on time charters are about these two clauses, so read them with the ship's real performance history in front of you.

A **trip time charter** (TCT) applies time-charter terms to one voyage. Period charters run for months or years. Time charters suit charterers with a regular programme, traders who want to control timing and optionality, and companies that want a known daily cost rather than a freight that moves with the spot market. The risk is the other side of that control: if the market falls or the ship sits idle, the hire is still due, and reletting the ship is your job.

## What is a contract of affreightment?

A COA is a promise by the owner to carry an agreed volume — a number of cargoes or a total tonnage — between agreed ranges over an agreed period. The owner chooses which ship performs each lifting, within the ship requirements in the contract. You get predictable capacity and freight without owning or operating ships; the owner gets a base load for the fleet.

The clauses that decide whether a COA works are:

- **Nomination periods**: when you must declare each cargo, and when the owner must name the ship.
- **Volume tolerances**: how far the total volume can move up or down, and at whose option.
- **Ship approval**: vetting standards and the process if a nominated ship is rejected.
- **Freight adjustment**: how the freight follows bunker prices or the Worldscale flat rate over the contract.
- **What happens when a cargo is not available**, or a ship cannot be found.

## How do you compare a voyage offer with a time charter?

Convert both to a **time-charter equivalent (TCE)**: the voyage freight minus the voyage costs (bunkers, port costs, dues and commissions), divided by the days the voyage takes. That gives a daily figure you can put next to a time-charter hire. We show a TCE with every freight idea; the [Worldscale and TCE guide](/blog/worldscale-and-tce-explained-how-tanker-freight-is-quoted) explains the method, and the [freight calculator](/freight-calculator) runs a first estimate with dated reference prices.

## How do charterers and owners usually choose?

- **Refiners and importers with steady flows** tend to cover the predictable part of their volume with a COA or period tonnage, and fix the rest spot.
- **Traders** work mostly spot, adding trip or short period charters when they want control of timing or a view on the freight market.
- **Owners** weigh the security of hire against spot exposure; an owner who expects a rising market prefers to stay spot.
- **LPG and ammonia** follow the same logic with their own terms; see [spot, time charter or COA for an LPG carrier](/blog/spot-time-charter-or-coa-how-to-charter-an-lpg-carrier).

What about a **bareboat charter**? There the charterer takes the ship without crew and runs it as if it were the owner, usually for years. It is a fleet and financing decision rather than a way to move a cargo, so we mention it only for completeness.

## What should you send the broker to get a recommendation?

{% callout label="Send the desk" linkHref="/contact" linkLabel="Send an inquiry" %}
Your volume profile (cargoes per month or year, and how firm they are), the load and discharge ranges, the flexibility you need on dates and quantity, how much freight risk you are prepared to carry, whether you have an operations team to run a ship on time charter, and the counterparty details we need for compliance screening.
{% /callout %}

With that, we can show you the structures side by side for your trade, in TCE terms, and the clauses we would negotiate first. The [step-by-step chartering guide](/guides/how-to-charter-a-tanker) explains what happens after you choose.
